Link shared extension modules against libpython on wasm64.

Upstream leaves LIBPYTHON empty on most platforms because ELF resolves an
extension module's Python symbols at dlopen time against the already-loaded
libpython (or the main executable). wasm has no such deferred resolution:
every import of a shared module must be satisfied by a module named on its
own link line, and the reactor toolchain's check-unresolved rejects a module
that links with unresolved env imports.

wasm64 therefore belongs in the same class as Android and Cygwin, which
upstream already handles here: MODULE_DEPS_SHARED gains $(LDLIBRARY) so
libpython3.14.so is built before any module links, and LIBPYTHON becomes
-lpython3.14 so MODULE_LDFLAGS_SHARED expands to $(BLDLIBRARY). Setting
LIBPYTHON rather than patching the link rules also keeps python-config,
python3.pc and sysconfig correct, so extension modules built later by pip
against the installed interpreter get the same link line.

--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -6466,7 +6466,11 @@
 # LIBPYTHON is used by python-config, python3.pc, the commands for building the
 # stdlib's own extension modules, and external package build systems via
 # sysconfig, so its value must be suitable for all those contexts.
-if test "$PY_ENABLE_SHARED" = "1" && ( test -n "$ANDROID_API_LEVEL" || test "$MACHDEP" = "cygwin"); then
+# wasm has no dlopen-time symbol resolution against the loaded interpreter, so
+# wasm64 needs the same treatment.
+if test "$PY_ENABLE_SHARED" = "1" && ( test -n "$ANDROID_API_LEVEL" \
+  || test "$MACHDEP" = "cygwin" \
+  || test "$host_cpu" = "wasm64" ); then
   MODULE_DEPS_SHARED="$MODULE_DEPS_SHARED \$(LDLIBRARY)"
   LIBPYTHON="-lpython${VERSION}${ABIFLAGS}"
 fi
